meta {
  name: Reconcile
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{groupAdmin}}/reconcile
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "path": "{{groupPrefix}}{{streamName}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("runs the leases of the group that path belongs to", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.body.prefix).to.equal(bru.getEnvVar("groupPrefix"));
    expect(res.body.failed || []).to.be.an("array").that.is.empty;
    expect(res.body.unread || []).to.be.an("array").that.is.empty;
  });
}

docs {
  # Reconcile

  Runs a group's leases against the relay again, named by a path rather than by a group key.

  This is what the relay's own read hook calls, which is why it takes no key: a starting read announces the path it is on, and the answer is a run against the leases the members themselves stated.
  It grants nothing a key would have bought, and a group with no live member answers `enforced: false` and is left alone.

  The answer is the run: `kicked` is every connection closed, each naming the stream inside the group, the leg it was on and its direction, and `kept` counts the ones a live member holds.
  `unread` appears where a per-protocol list would not answer, which means the sweep was partial and somebody may still be connected.
  `failed` appears where the relay refused to close one, which is a member still watching rather than a removal.

  Doing it by hand is how a run is watched without waiting for a reader to arrive.
  The hook is `deploy/reconcile-on-read.sh`, wired in `pathDefaults.runOnRead`.

  A path belonging to no group is refused with 400, a bare stream name naming no group.
}
